00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.242
And it completely undercuts

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:30.209
everything that Trump

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:31.978
and his legal team has said publicly

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:33.012
about the powers

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:34.247
that he had after

00:08:34.247 --> 00:08:35.114
leaving office

00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:36.949
or what he could do with these documents.

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:38.451
And I do think that is one of those

00:08:38.451 --> 00:08:39.352
damning lines

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.053
of the transcript of this tape

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:42.288
that we reported on.

00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:43.122
And there was a reason

00:08:43.122 --> 00:08:44.257
this tape has caused

00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:45.691
so much consternation

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:46.659
in Trump's legal team.

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:48.327
They didn't learn about it

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:49.262
all that long ago.

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:50.263
It was only in March

00:08:50.263 --> 00:08:52.131
when this recording was played

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:54.934
during testimony from another Trump aide

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:56.102
or they heard it.

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:57.303
And then Trump's legal team

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:59.672
was alerted to the fact that prosecutors

00:08:59.672 --> 00:09:02.041
did have this audio in their hands

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:04.076
and having Trump on tape admitting

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:05.778
that he doesn't have the power

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.647
to declassify it, which we all knew

00:09:07.647 --> 00:09:08.214
because we've read

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:09.382
the Presidential Records Act

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:10.182
we've looked at what

00:09:10.182 --> 00:09:12.318
that declassification process is.

00:09:12.318 --> 00:09:13.586
But it completely undercuts

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:14.921
any of the defenses

00:09:14.921 --> 00:09:15.855
that you've seen Trump's

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:17.490
legal team offer up publicly.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:18.891
And so that is why

00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:20.493
this is such a central piece

00:09:20.493 --> 00:09:21.327
in this reporting.

00:09:21.327 --> 00:09:23.095
Of course, in the bigger context

00:09:23.095 --> 00:09:23.763
of what we saw

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.231
last night with the former president

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:26.499
being indicted in this,

00:09:26.499 --> 00:09:27.600
of course, now going

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.735
where Caitlin Pawlenty's on Tuesday

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:31.170
to that Miami courthouse,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.405
this is going to be something

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:33.940
that is a real problem

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:34.974
for his legal team

00:09:34.974 --> 00:09:37.143
to try to defend in court and explain.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:38.244
And it's something that

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:39.278
they are pretty candid

00:09:39.278 --> 00:09:41.280
about behind the scenes when it comes to

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.349
just how crucial this tape is.

00:09:43.983 --> 00:09:47.820
Eliot, as a lawyer, when you look through

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.254
or listen

00:09:48.254 --> 00:09:49.589
to that transcript,

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:50.423
there's yes, him

00:09:50.423 --> 00:09:52.191
acknowledging that he can't

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:55.127
unilaterally declassify things by magic

00:09:55.561 --> 00:09:57.063
or after the fact.

00:09:57.063 --> 00:09:58.798
But he also seems to be holding

00:09:58.798 --> 00:10:01.667
and talking about a specific date.

00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:02.735
What stands out to you

00:10:02.735 --> 00:10:04.537
as most problematic

00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:06.038
for the former president's legal team?

00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:06.405
Well,

00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:06.806
that's

00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:07.607
what helps

00:10:07.607 --> 00:10:08.474
the former president

00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:08.741
is it

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:09.542
appears that they have not

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:10.443
found this document.

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:11.310
And it appears, based on

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.112
what we know about the charges thus far,

00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:14.614
that he was not charged

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:16.849
with disclosing the contents

00:10:16.849 --> 00:10:18.417
of a document to another person.

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:19.085
That itself

00:10:19.085 --> 00:10:20.152
would have been a federal offense

00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:22.622
if he had access to something sensitive

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:23.823
and showing it to somebody else

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:25.858
if it could hurt the national defense.

00:10:26.025 --> 00:10:28.861
Look, the a challenge here is that

00:10:29.195 --> 00:10:30.763
declassification declassification

00:10:30.763 --> 00:10:32.198
doesn't really matter

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:33.933
as an element of the offenses

00:10:33.933 --> 00:10:35.334
he's charged with,

00:10:35.334 --> 00:10:36.469
merely what it matters

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:38.237
if they are government records

00:10:38.237 --> 00:10:39.438
and most importantly,

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:40.373
that there was a series

00:10:40.373 --> 00:10:40.906
of acts

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:43.009
taken to obstruct the in the Justice

00:10:43.009 --> 00:10:44.243
Department's investigation.

00:10:44.243 --> 00:10:46.178
So it's a little bit of a red

00:10:46.178 --> 00:10:47.213
herring to go down the road

00:10:47.213 --> 00:10:48.080
of saying you know,

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.049
if he declassified it, therefore,

00:10:50.049 --> 00:10:50.983
there would not have been crimes

00:10:50.983 --> 00:10:51.417
committed here.

00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:52.451
Because the simple fact is,

00:10:52.451 --> 00:10:53.986
once the documents were removed,

00:10:53.986 --> 00:10:55.855
mishandled, misappropriated,

00:10:55.855 --> 00:10:57.189
whatever words you want to use,

00:10:57.189 --> 00:10:58.190
that's when the crimes were committed.

